        "math_academy_id": "q-66600",
        "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=1,$ then what is $3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-4\\pi )-4?$",
        "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=-2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x+2\\cdot (-2)\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-4\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=1,$ then $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-4\\pi )=1.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(x-4\\pi )-4=3(1)-4=3-4=-1.\n$$",
